---
// biome-ignore-all lint/correctness/noUnusedImports: Biome does not read an Astro template, so it cannot see the layout it renders.
// biome-ignore-all lint/correctness/noUnusedVariables: Biome does not read an Astro template, so it cannot see these.
import { MODEL_PRICES, PLAN_PRICES, PRICE_READ } from "@trajectonomics/data-collector/price-catalog"
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro"
import { planName } from "../lib/plans"
import { DATA_SOURCE_URL, SOURCE_URL } from "../lib/site"

const ESTIMATE_ADR_URL = `${SOURCE_URL}/blob/main/apps/data/docs/adr/0008-estimated-tiers-scale-a-measured-floor-by-a-stated-multiplier.md`
const PEAKS_ADR_URL = `${SOURCE_URL}/blob/main/apps/data/docs/adr/0009-peaks-count-cache-reads-apart-and-anchor-claude-sessions.md`
const COLLECTING_ADR_URL = `${SOURCE_URL}/blob/main/apps/data/docs/adr/0010-data-starts-when-collecting-starts.md`
const LIMITS_ADR_URL = `${SOURCE_URL}/blob/main/apps/data/docs/adr/0011-limits-are-estimated-from-the-usage-meter.md`
const LIMITS_CACHE_ADR_URL = `${SOURCE_URL}/blob/main/apps/data/docs/adr/0013-limits-are-priced-and-counted-with-cache.md`
const VALUE_ADR_URL = `${SOURCE_URL}/blob/main/apps/data/docs/adr/0012-prices-enter-with-a-dated-source.md`

const planPriceRows = Object.entries(PLAN_PRICES).flatMap(([plan, prices]) =>
	(prices ?? []).map((entry) => ({
		plan: planName(plan),
		tier: entry.tier,
		usd:
			entry.usdMonth === null
				? "not stated"
				: entry.usdMonth === 0
					? "free"
					: `$${entry.usdMonth} a month`,
		read: entry.source?.read ?? null,
		source: entry.source,
	})),
)

const modelPriceRows = Object.entries(MODEL_PRICES).map(([model, price]) => ({
	model,
	input: `$${price.input}`,
	output: `$${price.output}`,
	cacheRead: `$${price.cacheRead}`,
	cacheWrite: price.cacheWrite === null ? "input price" : `$${price.cacheWrite}`,
	read: price.source.read,
	source: price.source,
}))
---

<Layout
	title="Method · Trajectonomics Data"
	description="How the data site counts tokens, which tool feeds each plan, and what the numbers leave out."
	current="method"
>
	<article class="method">
		<p class="meta-label">Method</p>
		<h1 class="method-title">How we count</h1>
		<p class="method-lede">
			Tokens from the local logs of the AI coding tools we run, rolled up by UTC day.
		</p>

		<h2>The collector</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>The collector reads the local logs of the AI coding tools on our machines.</li>
			<li>
				It prints one total per plan, model and UTC hour, or one total per UTC day for the
				Mistral console export.
			</li>
			<li>
				Only those totals, a hashed machine label, plan and model ids, UTC buckets and numbers leave
				the machine. Prompts, session titles, project names, paths and account ids never do.
			</li>
			<li>
				Writes are idempotent upserts keyed by machine, plan, model and UTC bucket, so a missed
				hour heals on the next run.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>Which tool feeds each plan</h2>
		<table class="source-table">
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th scope="col">Source</th>
					<th scope="col">Plan</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr>
					<td>Claude Code session logs</td>
					<td>Claude</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>OpenCode's local database</td>
					<td>OpenCode Go, OpenCode (free)</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Antigravity's <code>transcript.jsonl</code></td>
					<td>Antigravity</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Cursor's agent store</td>
					<td>Cursor</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>T3 Code provider logs</td>
					<td>Meta (Muse), and Cursor when its agent store has no usage</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Mistral console CSV export</td>
					<td>Mistral, one row per day</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Pi session logs</td>
					<td>The plan named by each record's provider id</td>
				</tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>

		<h2>What is excluded</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>
				Hourly rows are not public: the API serves daily rollups, and one aggregate per plan
				computed from the hourly rows. A response carries the UTC day a peak's window starts,
				never an hour.
			</li>
			<li>
				Prompts, session titles, project names, paths, account ids and times of day are excluded
				from every published response; a response carries no machine or contributor.
			</li>
			<li>
				A Cursor run is counted once: its agent store when <code>usage_json</code> is populated,
				else T3 Code's Cursor logs.
			</li>
			<li>
				Antigravity's duplicate full transcript is skipped. A step with no model takes its
				conversation's model, else <code>unknown-gemini</code>; that step counts in the plan total and
				not in a per-model row.
			</li>
			<li>
				Claude streaming repeats one request's usage several times, so one row is kept per
				<code>requestId</code>. Claude's <code>&lt;synthetic&gt;</code> models are skipped.
			</li>
			<li>T3 Code's <code>claudeAgent</code> lines are ignored; Claude Code's own logs are the source.</li>
			<li>
				The Mistral daily export has no in/out split, so those cells say <code>n/a</code>.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>UTC days</h2>
		<p>
			A day runs from 00:00 to 24:00 UTC. A row that starts at 23:00 UTC belongs to that UTC day,
			whatever the local clock says.
		</p>

		<h2 id="plans">Limits</h2>
		<p>
			“Limits” shows the most tokens a plan carried in a window, with cache reads counted
			apart: they cost far less, so they never lift the figure. Missing hours and days count as
			zero, so a window is calendar-consecutive, not consecutive in the rows. Claude's 5 hours
			start at its first active hour, because its session starts with the first message; other
			plans' windows slide over the clock. Claude and Antigravity state no monthly window, so
			their 30-day figure is the week × 30/7. Every figure is what we used, never the plan's
			limit. The reasoning is in <a href={PEAKS_ADR_URL}>ADR 0009</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Limits from the meter</h2>
		<p>
			Claude and OpenCode Go publish their own usage meter, and the collector reads it every hour.
			A window's 100% is estimated from how far that meter moved against the tokens we used
			meanwhile: tokens used ÷ percentage points moved × 100. Consecutive readings that share a
			reset time form one segment; a reset, a change of reset time or a fall in the percentage
			starts a new one. Each reading is rounded to a whole percent, so the range widens by one
			point per segment. Only the estimate, its range and its evidence leave the Worker, never a
			reading, its time or its reset time.
		</p>
		<p>
			The estimate comes in three units. Tokens leaves cache reads out, as the peaks do. With cache
			counts them in full. API $ prices every token kind at its model's list price per million, so
			cache reads count at their real weight; an hour holding a model with no list price makes that
			hour's dollars unknown, so the window has no dollar figure. An estimate is rough until it has
			three points of meter over at least 24 hours. The reasoning is in
			<a href={LIMITS_ADR_URL}>ADR 0011</a> and <a href={LIMITS_CACHE_ADR_URL}>ADR 0013</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Estimated tiers</h2>
		<p>
			We measure one tier per plan. Another tier is estimated from a tier we measured times the
			multiplier its vendor states, and 30 days use the weekly multiplier. An estimate is a lower
			bound, not the plan's limit; a tier whose multiplier the vendor does not state shows “–”.
			The reasoning is in <a href={ESTIMATE_ADR_URL}>ADR 0008</a>.
		</p>

		<h2 id="value">Value</h2>
		<p>
			“Value” plots tokens per $1 against API value per $1, one dot per plan with a weekly figure
			and a price. The weekly price is the monthly USD price × 12 / 52. Tokens per $1 is the
			week's tokens ÷ the weekly price. API value per $1 is the week's tokens × the blended API
			list price of the plan's own mix ÷ the weekly price.
		</p>
		<p>
			The blend prices what we used on each model at that model's list price, adds it up, and
			divides by the tokens without cache reads. A model with no price stays out of the blend; a
			plan whose models all lack one has no dot. A cache write with no stated price is priced as
			input. A free plan has no price to divide by, and a plan whose weekly figure is “most we
			used” rather than an estimate gets a hollow dot. The shaded area is above the median on both
			axes, or the upper-right third with fewer than three dots.
		</p>

		<h2 id="tokens">Cache</h2>
		<p>
			The bars split each plan's tokens into input, output, cache read and cache write. Cache rate
			is cache read ÷ all tokens; a source that reports no cache split says so and has no dot in
			the cache chart. The cache chart plots tokens per $1 against cache rate from 0 to 100%, one
			dot per priced plan with a cache split.
		</p>
		<p>
			The reasoning behind per-dollar value is in <a href={VALUE_ADR_URL}>ADR 0012</a>.
		</p>

		<h2 id="prices">Prices</h2>
		<p>
			A price enters only with a dated public source: a URL, the day it was read and the sentence
			it states. A missing price stays missing and is never inferred from a neighbouring tier or
			from a multiplier. These were all read on {PRICE_READ}.
		</p>

		<h3>Plans</h3>
		<div class="price-table-wrap">
			<table class="source-table price-table">
				<thead>
					<tr>
						<th scope="col">Plan</th>
						<th scope="col">Tier</th>
						<th scope="col">USD</th>
						<th scope="col">Read</th>
						<th scope="col">Source</th>
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					{
						planPriceRows.map((row) => (
							<tr>
								<td>{row.plan}</td>
								<td>{row.tier}</td>
								<td>{row.usd}</td>
								<td>{row.read ?? "–"}</td>
								<td>
									{row.source === null ? (
										<span>free or not stated</span>
									) : (
										<a href={row.source.url} title={row.source.quote}>
											{row.source.url}
										</a>
									)}
								</td>
							</tr>
						))
					}
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>

		<h3>Models</h3>
		<p>
			List prices in US dollars per million tokens. A cache write of “input price” uses the input
			price.
		</p>
		<div class="price-table-wrap">
			<table class="source-table price-table">
				<thead>
					<tr>
						<th scope="col">Model</th>
						<th scope="col">Input</th>
						<th scope="col">Output</th>
						<th scope="col">Cache read</th>
						<th scope="col">Cache write</th>
						<th scope="col">Read</th>
						<th scope="col">Source</th>
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					{
						modelPriceRows.map((row) => (
							<tr>
								<td>
									<code>{row.model}</code>
								</td>
								<td>{row.input}</td>
								<td>{row.output}</td>
								<td>{row.cacheRead}</td>
								<td>{row.cacheWrite}</td>
								<td>{row.read}</td>
								<td>
									<a href={row.source.url} title={row.source.quote}>
										{row.source.url}
									</a>
								</td>
							</tr>
						))
					}
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>

		<h3>Formulas</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>Weekly price = monthly USD × 12 / 52.</li>
			<li>Tokens per $1 = weekly tokens ÷ weekly price.</li>
			<li>
				Blended API $ per token = Σ over priced models of the tokens in, out, cache read and
				cache write at list prices ÷ Σ tokens without cache reads.
			</li>
			<li>API value per $1 = weekly tokens × blended API $ per token ÷ weekly price.</li>
			<li>Cache rate = cache read ÷ all tokens.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>What “measured by us” means</h2>
		<p>
			Every row here belongs to the <code>measured-by-us</code> series: our collector counted it
			from our own usage records. It is not a vendor invoice and not a plan's own meter. Meta rows
			are marked best-effort. Mistral rows come from its daily console export.
		</p>
		<p>
			Collection starts at the first hour a collector runs, and the site shows only usage the
			collector measured live, never a backfilled history. A window longer than what we have
			collected shows its days so far instead of a figure. The reasoning is in
			<a href={COLLECTING_ADR_URL}>ADR 0010</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>The asOf view</h2>
		<p>
			Add <code>?asOf=YYYY-MM-DD</code> to the overview to compute every figure from data up to and
			including that day. It is a view computed at request time, not a frozen snapshot: a late batch
			or a correction can change it. Each response names its method version, and the overview shows
			the version it used.
		</p>

		<h2>Quota numbers</h2>
		<p>
			Quota percentages exist only for plans with a readable meter. Claude and OpenCode Go are the
			plans whose meter we can read, so they are the plans whose 100% is estimated. A plan with no
			meter means no quota number on this site.
		</p>

		<div class="method-repo">
			<p>
				The collector, the Worker and this page are in <a href={DATA_SOURCE_URL}>apps/data</a> in
				the <a href={SOURCE_URL}>public repository</a>.
			</p>
		</div>
	</article>
</Layout>
